  "The Maya Civilization": {
    page1: "The Maya Civilization developed advanced writing, mathematics, and astronomy in Central America, flourishing across present-day Mexico, Guatemala, Belize, and Honduras from roughly 2000 BCE through the Spanish conquest in the 1500s CE. Unlike a single unified empire, the Maya lived in numerous independent city-states, such as Tikal, Calakmul, and Chichen Itza, that shared a common culture, religion, and writing system while often competing and warring with one another. Maya scholars developed one of the most sophisticated writing systems in the ancient Americas, along with remarkably accurate calendars and astronomical observations. The Maya Civilization's achievements in mathematics, astronomy, and architecture continue to fascinate historians and remain visible today in the ruins of their great cities.",
    page2: "The Maya Civilization reached its height during the Classic Period, roughly 250 to 900 CE, when city-states like Tikal and Calakmul built towering stone pyramids, palaces, and temples dedicated to their gods. Maya society was highly stratified, with powerful kings and priests at the top claiming divine authority, supported by nobles, skilled artisans, merchants, and a large population of farmers who grew maize, beans, and squash. Maya cities engaged in frequent warfare with neighboring city-states, seeking captives for sacrifice and control over valuable resources and trade routes. Despite constant political rivalry, Maya city-states remained culturally connected through shared religious beliefs, writing, and calendar systems.",
    page3: "Maya scribes developed a complex hieroglyphic writing system combining symbols for whole words and syllables, which they carved into stone monuments and painted onto bark-paper books called codices. This writing system recorded historical events, royal genealogies, and religious rituals, though the Spanish destroyed nearly all Maya codices during colonization, leaving only four known books to survive. Maya mathematicians independently developed the concept of zero and used a base-twenty number system, allowing them to make precise astronomical calculations centuries before many other civilizations. Maya astronomers tracked the movements of the sun, moon, and Venus with remarkable accuracy, using this knowledge to create complex calendar systems, including the famous Long Count calendar.",
    page4: "The Maya built massive stepped pyramids topped with temples, using these structures for religious ceremonies, royal burials, and astronomical observation, aligning many buildings precisely with solar and celestial events. Maya religion was deeply polytheistic, involving numerous gods associated with nature, agriculture, and the heavens, and rulers performed bloodletting rituals and human sacrifice to maintain favor with these deities. Maya cities also featured ballcourts where players competed in a ritual ball game with religious and political significance, sometimes ending in the sacrifice of the losing team's captain. Skilled Maya artisans produced intricate pottery, jade carvings, and textiles that were traded throughout Mesoamerica alongside cacao, obsidian, and other valuable goods.",
    page5: "Maya kings claimed divine right to rule, often depicted in art performing bloodletting rituals to communicate with gods and ancestors on behalf of their people. Priests held enormous influence, since they alone understood the complex calendar and astronomical calculations needed to schedule religious ceremonies and agricultural activities. Scribes, a specially trained and respected class, recorded history and religious knowledge using the hieroglyphic writing system, ensuring important information was preserved for future generations. Farmers made up the vast majority of Maya society, using intensive agricultural techniques, including raised fields and terracing, to grow enough food to support the large urban populations of Maya cities.",
    page6: "Around 900 CE, many great Maya cities in the southern lowlands were mysteriously abandoned, a decline historians attribute to a combination of prolonged drought, warfare, overpopulation, and environmental strain on agricultural resources. Despite this collapse, Maya civilization did not disappear entirely, as northern cities like Chichen Itza continued to thrive for centuries afterward, and Maya peoples and their descendants still live in the region today, speaking Mayan languages and preserving many traditions. Spanish conquistadors did not fully conquer the last independent Maya kingdom until 1697, showing the remarkable resilience of Maya culture. Modern archaeologists continue deciphering Maya hieroglyphics and studying Maya ruins, revealing new insights into this sophisticated ancient civilization.",
    page7: "The Maya Civilization developed advanced writing, mathematics, and astronomy across numerous independent city-states in Central America, reaching its height during the Classic Period from roughly 250 to 900 CE. Maya scribes created a complex hieroglyphic writing system, while Maya astronomers and mathematicians developed precise calendars and the concept of zero centuries before many other civilizations. Around 900 CE, many southern Maya cities were abandoned due to drought, warfare, and environmental strain, though Maya culture persisted for centuries afterward in the north. Maya descendants still live in the region today, and their remarkable achievements in writing, mathematics, and astronomy remain a lasting testament to this sophisticated ancient civilization."
  }
